from pathlib import Path
import json,numpy as np
import matplotlib
matplotlib.use('Agg')
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
O=Path(__file__).parent
variants=[('Coupled baseline','effective-flight-fit.json'),('No brake threshold','effective-flight-no-threshold-fit.json'),('Constant lift coefficient','constant-lift-fit.json'),('Manual-phase wind','phase-wind-flight-fit.json'),('Unequal brake gain','brake-gain-flight-fit.json'),('Centripetal restoring','centripetal-flight-fit.json'),('Aero stiffness/damping','aero-bank-flight-fit.json'),('Onset by loading','load-threshold-flight-fit.json'),('Onset + nonlinear bank','tan-bank-flight-fit.json')]
fig,axes=plt.subplots(1,3,figsize=(16,6))
for ax,(key,title) in zip(axes,[('bank_rmse_deg','Mean bank RMS error (deg)'),('course_final_error_deg','Mean absolute final course error (deg)'),('climb_rmse','Mean climb RMS error (m/s)')]):
 vals=[]
 for name,file in variants:
  a=json.loads((O/file).read_text());vals.append(np.mean([abs(r[key]) for r in a['validation']]))
 ax.barh(np.arange(len(vals)),vals,color=['#244c65']+['#a6b9c5']*8);ax.set_yticks(np.arange(len(vals)),[v[0] for v in variants] if ax==axes[0] else []);ax.invert_yaxis();ax.set_xlabel(title);ax.grid(axis='x',alpha=.2)
 for i,v in enumerate(vals):ax.text(v,i,f' {v:.2f}',va='center',fontsize=9)
 ax.set_xlim(0,max(vals)*1.2)
fig.suptitle('Controlled model comparisons: no candidate resolves every output',fontsize=16);fig.tight_layout(rect=(0,0,1,.94));fig.savefig(O/'iteration-coupled-ablation.png',dpi=150);plt.close(fig)
p=O/'physics-development.html';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
section='''<section id="coupled-diagnostics"><h2>What the next comparisons ruled out</h2><figure><img src="iteration-coupled-ablation.png" alt="Bank, course and climb errors for nine coupled-model variants"><figcaption>Each bar averages the eight repeatedly inspected three-second development cases. Most variants use the same 53 training windows and wind assumptions; the manual-phase-wind variant deliberately changes those wind assumptions. Mean errors summarize tradeoffs and can conceal individual failures. None is accepted for controller design.</figcaption></figure><p>Removing brake thresholds helps some Trout Lake cases but loses the Hood River tight turn. Unequal left/right gain, a centripetal restoring term, and aerodynamic scaling of bank stiffness/damping do not resolve the shared-fit problem. Allowing effective left-brake onset to differ between the two loading configurations improves early Trout Lake predictions to about 1.5–1.9 degrees bank RMS, but its late high-bank prediction fails at 18.5 degrees RMS. A nonlinear bank restoring law reduces that failure to 12.3 degrees while worsening Hood River turn continuation. These are tests of effective response hypotheses, not measurements of slack or proof that ballast changed brake geometry. All preserve the audited per-flight PWM ranges.</p><figure><img src="iteration-force-inversion.png" alt="Flight-implied lift and drag coefficients against payload angle and estimated airspeed"><figcaption>Forces reconstructed from 0.2-second-smoothed EKF velocity derivatives, the assumed flight masses and winds, and the baseline fitted thrust law. Color increases from purple to yellow with absolute payload bank (0–50 degrees). Lift here denotes the magnitude of aerodynamic force perpendicular to estimated air velocity and can include side force. It is not a direct canopy lift measurement.</figcaption></figure><pre>F_a = m (dv_ground/dt − g_NED) − T e_thrust
D = −F_a · e_air
F_perpendicular = F_a + D e_air
C_L,effective = |F_perpendicular| / (½ ρ S V_air²)
C_D,effective = D / (½ ρ S V_air²)</pre><p>Payload pitch minus flight-path angle has little pooled linear correlation with reconstructed lift coefficient (Hood River +0.035, Trout Lake −0.067). This weakens its use as an instantaneous canopy-angle proxy; it does not establish a new lift law. The strong coefficient-versus-speed trend partly follows algebraically from dividing an approximately weight-supporting force by V², and must not be fitted as independent aerodynamic evidence. A constant-lift-coefficient candidate was tested and did not improve overall prediction.</p><p>Wind estimated from moderate-bank manual-flight samples, excluding the displayed development windows, differs from the earlier steady-flight estimate. The coupled refit still fails Trout Lake, so that wind adjustment alone is not a solution. Crossflow residuals of roughly 0.8–0.9 m/s in early manual flight also show that constant wind plus negligible sideslip is an imperfect assumption. These estimates remain sensitivity cases.</p><p>Next: separate canopy force response from payload attitude, identify the longitudinal response over longer records, and test the resulting shared model on complete turn sequences. Three-second successes alone cannot establish a sustained-radius envelope. Sources and results are the <code>fit_*_flight.py</code> scripts, matching <code>*-flight-fit.json</code> files, <code>diagnose_force_inversion.py</code>, and <code>manual-phase-wind.json</code>.</p></section>'''
if 'id="coupled-diagnostics"' not in s:p.write_text(s.replace('</html>',section+'</html>'),encoding='utf-8')
print('Wrote comparison plot and journal entry')
